I made a really stupid mistake in my current game.  This is a hobbit 
monk (having named him Luther, I actually wanted not to make him a 
doppelganger but a human; somehow, hitting "h" only resulted in his 
becoming a hobbit) and the trouble is that my game does not respect a 
showrace command in the defaults.nh file, so I would have to set that 
option each time I fire up the game again to remind myself that my 
character is a hobbit, not a human.  Of course, I forgot doing so after 
two or three continuations of the game.  The danger of genociding my own 
race was always small, because I have become aware of it, and I never 
genocide "h" since that time (just two subspecies of "h", spending two 
uncursed scrolls of genocide, instead of one blessed one).

However, I did not think of another danger, that of sacrificing your own 
race.  It would not normally have happened, because all hobbits are 
created peacefully.  Now my god gave me a pet movanic deva for one 
sacrifice.  The next application of my bag of tricks created a hobbit, 
my deva killed him.  Not considering a hobbit a human (I am aware that 
sacrificing a human is a bad thing in most cases), I put him on the 
altar and #offered him.  Of course, I angered my god and took a wisdom 
penalty, which my unicorn horn cured.  The problem is that the altar was 
converted to chaotic and if I start sacrificing on this altar, it is 
probably *me* that will be converted, rendering the game unwinnable, 
since I have not yet done the quest.  Even if I did the quest first, I 
would not like to convert myself, since I have created Excalibur as an 
insurance against wands of draining...  On the other hand, I do not know 
of any other way of appeasing my god than sacrificing on a coaligned 
altar.  The mine-town altar is cross-aligned, and down to level 20, I 
have not found another altar besides the one that just turned chaotic.

Is there a way of converting the altar back despite the anger of my god? 
  Or do I have to wait until I find another lawful altar (which may not 
happen at all)?  Will waiting a few thousand turns (and killing many 
monsters to improve alignment, throwing gems at unicorns to improve 
luck) be helpful?  Or have I just committed myself to atheism for the 
rest of the game?

